00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:16.489
“It’ll be a cold day in hell before I
let twins fly long-haul over-water routes.”

00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:21.310
Those were the words of Lynn Helms—administrator
of the Federal Aviation Administration during

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.340
the Reagan administration.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.560
At the time, no commercial american airplane
with two engines was allowed to fly anywhere

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.599
farther than 60 minutes from a diversion airport.

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:35.530
The belief was that, if one engine failed,
the other could only safely fly the plane

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:40.660
for about an hour, but this rule severely
limited what smaller planes could do.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.780
On North Atlantic routes like New York to
London, twin-engine planes could only fly

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.390
in these areas but a direct route looked like
this.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.539
The options were to either fly a twin-engine
plane on an inefficient routing or fly a inefficient

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:55.030
three or four engine plane.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.960
There was no place for long-and-skinny routes
between smaller cities using smaller planes

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.699
since airline couldn’t legally fly those
smaller planes.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:08.090
This one simple rule changed the very way
airplanes were built.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.990
Now, in the 60’s, this 60 minute regulation
only applied to planes with two engines.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:18.050
Of course aircraft manufacturers could build
quad-engine jets but those had to be huge

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.270
for airlines to make their money’s worth
with their high fuel consumption.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.369
The 747’s of the time could carry more than
400 passengers.

00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:31.780
They could therefore only fly on super high-demand
routes like New York to London to have any

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.390
hope of being full.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.950
In order to start flying more convenient non-stop
routes from smaller markets, planes had to

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.810
get smaller while still being legally allowed
to hop the pond.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.109
That’s where trijets came into play.

00:01:44.109 --> 00:01:48.670
With three engines, these planes weren’t
subject to the same 60-minute regulation as

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:49.670
twinjets.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.310
They could easily fly any transatlantic route.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.729
That’s why in the 70s or 80s, the long-haul
jets you’d see at airpots were, for the

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:01.159
most part, either 747’s or trijets like
the DC-10.

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:05.619
This 60-minute regulation was inconvenient
for Atlantic Crossings, but in the Pacific

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:09.720
it actually changed how Hawaii developed.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.750
There are zero diversion airports between
California and Hawaii so the route isn’t

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.680
even close to covered under the 60-minute
rule.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.020
As a result, airlines could only fly huge
planes between the mainland and Hawaii which

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.500
meant that planes could pretty much only fly
to Honolulu.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.250
There was virtually no service between the
other islands and the mainland which meant

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.920
the other islands were severely isolated.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.130
That’s part of the reason why the tourism
industry only picked up on the other islands

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:37.380
in recent decades.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.390
Luckily, change was coming.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:42.860
The 60 minute rule originated from the days
of piston driven propeller aircraft.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.660
With these, it was far more common for engines
to just stop working mid-flight.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.330
That’s why there were contingency engines.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.320
The regulations just didn’t adapt to the
increased reliability of jet engines.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:01.250
Statistically, for every failure of a jet
engine, there are 117 piston engine failures.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:07.120
Once the jet age rolled in, engine failure
just wasn’t as much of a concern, so, in

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.430
1985, the FAA begrudgingly granted permission
to Trans World Airlines to fly their twin-engined

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:20.090
767 direct between Boston and Paris—a route
taking it up to 120 minutes away from diversion

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:21.090
airports.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:26.190
This was the first example of a brand new
FAA certification called ETOPS—“Extended-range

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:31.450
Twin-engine Operational Performance Standards,”
or more colloquially, “engines turn or passengers

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:32.450
swim.”

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.480
Before an airline can fly a long over-water
route they have to buy a plane with what’s

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.700
known as an ETOPS type rating.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.430
Basically that means that the plane was built
with adequate redundancies, communications

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.950
systems, and fire suppression systems to fly
safely if one engine fails.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:51.840
For example, the 767—the first plane to
get an ETOPS certification—has a type rating

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.920
of 180 minutes meaning it can fly anywhere
as long as its 180 minutes from a diversion

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:58.920
airport.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.710
But just because a plane has a type rating
doesn’t mean an airline can fly it ETOPS.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:07.870
They have to have a special maintenance plan,
a special flight crew, special cabin crew,

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:13.000
special dispatchers, special fuel quantities,
and special passenger recovery plans since,

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.639
just because there’s a runway doesn’t
mean that a plane can safely divert since

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:20.759
the emergency doesn’t end once the plane
lands.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:25.509
Cold Bay, Alaska, for example, is a perfect
diversion airport for routes between Asia

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:26.759
and North America.

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:31.389
It only has six commercial flights per week
nowadays but as a former Air Force Base it

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:33.440
has an enormous runway.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.841
The only issue is that the town of Cold Bay
has a population of 108—its tiny—so any

00:04:39.841 --> 00:04:44.870
diversions automatically double or triple
the amount of people in the small town.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:49.110
There certainly aren’t enough hotel rooms
or restaurants to house and feed stranded

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:54.580
passengers so, if airlines plan to use Cold
Bay as a diversion airport, they need to make

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:00.360
a plan for how to house, feed, and recover
passengers within 48 hours of landing.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.610
Last year an American Airlines 787 was flying
from Shanghai to Chicago when its right engine

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.470
had an issue halfway across the Pacific Ocean.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.830
The plane quickly took a left turn diverting
to Cold Bay.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:17.090
Even before landing the plan was implemented
as flight attendants served a second meal

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.469
service early.

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:22.639
Just a few hours after safely landing in Cold
Bay, American’s mechanics took off from

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:27.370
Seattle bound for Cold Bay to start fixing
the plane while Alaska Airlines, American’s

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:31.389
partner, sent a 737 from Anchorage to pick
up the stranded passengers.

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:35.319
Meanwhile, flight attendants served the third
set of meals they had stocked while waiting

00:05:35.319 --> 00:05:39.600
on the ground and the coast guard opened their
heated hanger to passengers.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.550
Just 10 hours after the emergency landing,
passengers were on their way to Anchorage

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.509
where they spent the night before taking an
American 757 to Chicago.

00:05:48.509 --> 00:05:52.620
That was a perfect example of how the passenger
recovery plan worked.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.620
The quick response and defined plan helped
the airline get passengers out safely and

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:58.620
quickly.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.550
Now, because of the solid engine reliability,
numerous redundancies, and well-designed passenger

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:07.790
recovery plans, airlines and airplanes can
now receive insane ETOPS certifications.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:16.259
The 787 Dreamliner, the plane that diverted
to Cold Bay, has a type rating of 330 minutes.

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:21.229
That means it can fly up to 5.5 hours away
from a diversion airport.

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:25.069
Certain routes over long-ocean stretches in
the southern hemisphere were theoretically

00:06:25.069 --> 00:06:30.310
possible in the past with four engine planes
but were economically impossible since airlines

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:35.830
could never fill the large planes on the low-demand
city pairs like Melbourne to Santiago.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:42.370
With the ETOPS 330 certification, LATAM Airlines
can fly their small 787 economically on this

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:45.660
relatively low-demand route across the South
Pacific.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:53.610
The Airbus a350 is even rated for ETOPS 370—it
can fly 6 hours and 10 minutes away from diversion

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:54.610
airports.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:59.910
This plane can therefore fly everywhere on
earth except directly over the South Pole.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:04.460
Because of this simple rule change, three
and four engine planes are largely a relic

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:05.539
of the past.

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:10.169
Boeing and Airbus’ largest jets are both
their only four engine planes in production—the

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:12.620
747 and a380.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:17.590
Nearly all North Atlantic traffic today is
on twin-engined planes as smaller and smaller

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:19.400
planes get ETOPS certifications.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:26.710
Air Canada, for example, flies their tiny
120 passenger a319 with ETOPS certification

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:30.029
daily between St Johns Airport and London
Heathrow.

00:07:30.029 --> 00:07:36.229
British Airways even sends the even smaller
a318 between New York and London City Airport.

00:07:36.229 --> 00:07:40.830
These routes would have been unimaginable
30 years ago but the reliability of the airplanes

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:46.460
of today mean we need not fear flying small
planes over big oceans.

